{"parentUuid":"5992dc71-cfdc-47d4-b189-7d3fcb9d93f4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01XWZ8BXogTkySnpB55Xb3Pg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good catch — the install hint shouldn't nag about something the user already has. Small extension to the data model:\n\n### Install hints: list per agent + `ProvidesAny` suppression\n\n```go\ntype installHint struct {\n    Message     string   // display text\n    ProvidesAny []string // if ANY of these skill names show up in discovery,\n                         // suppress this hint (already installed)\n}\n\nvar installHints = map[string][]installHint{\n    \"claude-code\": {\n        {Message: \"Install `pr-review-toolkit` via `claude plugin install entireio/pr-review-toolkit`\",\n         ProvidesAny: []string{\"/pr-review-toolkit:review-pr\", \"/pr-review-toolkit:code-reviewer\"}},\n        {Message: \"Install `test-auditor` via the superpowers plugin\",\n         ProvidesAny: []string{\"/test-auditor\"}},\n    },\n    \"codex\": {\n        {Message: \"Install `codex-review-pack` via `codex plugins add …`\",\n         ProvidesAny: []string{\"/codex:adversarial-review\"}},\n    },\n    \"gemini-cli\": {\n        {Message: \"Install `gemini-code-review` via `gemini extensions install <url>`\",\n         ProvidesAny: nil}, // no known skill name to detect; always show\n    },\n}\n```\n\n**Render rule:** for each agent, iterate `installHints[agent]`; drop any where `ProvidesAny` intersects the discovered skills set; render the rest as `huh.NewNote` lines in the \"Install more\" section. If all are suppressed, the whole \"Install more\" section is omitted (not even a heading).\n\n### Dedupe\n\nWhile we're here: deduplicate by skill name across built-in + discovered, built-in wins. In practice they don't live in the same place on disk, but a cheap `map[string]struct{}` check defends against future refactors where a \"built-in\" is actually shipped as a bundled skill file (Claude Code already does this for `/simplify` — it's in the plugin cache tree, but it ships with the binary). If the discovery scan happens to also find it, we'd show the built-in-with-description once, not twice.\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n`ProvidesAny` is a small form of **declarative suppression** — the registry says \"I know what this plugin provides; hide me if you see any of it.\" It's better than writing logic like `if pluginInstalled(\"pr-review-toolkit\")` because plugin detection is fuzzy per agent, but \"do any of these specific skill names appear in the discovered set?\" is trivial. A design principle worth reusing: when a condition is hard to detect directly, list the observable fingerprint it leaves and match on that.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\nSection 1 complete. Moving to Section 2.\n\n---\n\n## Section 2 of 3 — Discovery algorithm\n\n### The `SkillDiscoverer` adapter, per agent\n\n```go\n// in cmd/entire/cli/agent/agent.go\ntype SkillDiscoverer interface {\n    Agent\n    DiscoverReviewSkills(ctx context.Context) ([]DiscoveredSkill, error)\n}\n\ntype DiscoveredSkill struct {\n    Name        string // e.g. \"/pr-review-toolkit:review-pr\"\n    Description string // scraped from SKILL.md frontmatter if available, else \"\"\n    SourcePath  string // disk path — for debug logging; not shown in picker\n}\n```\n\n### Per-agent scan rules (what \"installed\" means)\n\n| Agent | Scan root | Skill entry shape | Skill name derivation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Claude Code** | `~/.claude/plugins/cache/<marketplace>/<plugin>/<version>/skills/<skill>/SKILL.md` + `~/.claude/skills/<skill>/SKILL.md` (user skills) | one directory = one skill; YAML frontmatter in SKILL.md | `/<plugin>:<skill>` for plugin skills, `/<skill>` for user skills |\n| **Codex** | `~/.codex/plugins/<plugin>/` (per `/plugins` built-in) — **exact subdir layout needs a codex-rs source check before implementation; spec pins \"verify before coding\"** | TBD — probably `SKILL.md` or TOML manifest | `/<plugin>:<skill>` is the current Codex convention observed in `cmd/entire/cli/agent/codex/` |\n| **Gemini CLI** | Gemini extensions path (TBD — needs lookup on `.gemini/extensions/` or equivalent) | per Gemini extension manifest format | `/<extension-command>` |\n\nImplementation strategy: ship Claude Code's discoverer first (best-known layout, plus the main user base). Codex and Gemini discoverers return empty slice + nil error initially, meaning built-in + install hint is the full experience for them until their scan paths are verified. The interface is in place, so adding a real implementation later is drop-in.\n\n### Match rule — \"review-adjacent\"\n\nCase-insensitive substring match on **either** skill name **or** SKILL.md frontmatter `description:` field, against a fixed keyword set:\n\n```go\nvar reviewKeywords = []string{\n    \"review\", \"audit\", \"inspect\", \"critique\",\n    \"assess\", \"security-scan\", \"lint\",\n}\n```\n\nSo `/test-auditor` matches on name (`audit`); `/pr-review-toolkit:silent-failure-hunter` matches on description (frontmatter says \"find silent failures in code review\"); `/codex:adversarial-review` matches on name.\n\n**Why not require an explicit frontmatter tag?** Because we don't control skill authors' frontmatter, and the current crop of real review skills doesn't use one. Adding a requirement would hide skills until their authors opt in. Keyword substring catches the existing universe and doesn't gate on cooperation.\n\n**Why cap the keyword list?** Keeps discovery predictable. Users can still manually add any skill to `settings.json` — discovery is a convenience, not a gate.\n\n### Caching\n\nDiscovery runs only inside the picker (on `entire review` first-run or `--edit`), not on every hook fire. For a user with 50 plugin skills, worst-case scan is ~50 file reads — well under 100ms on any modern disk. No cache needed; simpler is better.\n\n### Dedicated package or inline?\n\nNew package `cmd/entire/cli/agent/skilldiscovery/` (thin, just the interface + keyword list + match helper). Each agent package (claudecode, codex, geminicli) implements `DiscoverReviewSkills` on its agent type. Lets the review.go side import only `skilldiscovery.Matches()` and the agent adapter — no direct coupling to `~/.claude/...` paths.\n\n### Graceful failure\n\n`DiscoverReviewSkills` returns `(skills, error)`. Any error is logged at `Debug` and discovery is treated as \"found nothing\" — never blocks the picker. Concrete failure modes we plan for:\n\n- Agent's plugin dir doesn't exist (fresh install, no plugins yet) → returns nil, nil (not an error).\n- One malformed SKILL.md → skip that skill, log, continue.\n- Permission error reading a plugin dir → skip, log, continue.\n- Entire home directory inaccessible → returns nil, nil (benign — treat as empty).\n\n---\n\nDoes section 2 (discovery contract, scan rules, keyword-based match) read right before I move into section 3 — picker UX flow, runtime re-verification at spawn time, and what happens on the error paths (empty picker, missing skill at spawn, backward-compat with 1009's settings shape)?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2631,"cache_read_input_tokens":162498,"output_tokens":4523,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2631,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":4523,"cache_read_input_tokens":162498,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2631,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2631},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaKMYCnhUXcgRPwD6NxhG","type":"assistant","uuid":"43e1be4b-bb2e-401f-a61a-d59600674714","timestamp":"2026-04-22T19:24:15.726Z","userType":"external","entrypoi