{"parentUuid":"abe29e66-4acc-475f-9322-a8905240a8c8","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01SnesT8Xag1DnADncFjeEHf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018Cr4JSErfAQhFkbyhE9rEd","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/entire-review/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-22-entire-review-spawn-agent-picker-design.md","content":"# `entire review` — spawn-time agent picker (Sub-phase 2.1)\n\n**Status:** design — awaiting implementation plan\n**Date:** 2026-04-22\n**Author:** Peyton Montei (with Claude Opus 4.7)\n**Base branch:** `soph/entire-review-2` (PR #1009, now includes Phase 1 picker-install work)\n\n## Problem\n\nToday, when `entire review` is run in a repo with multiple agents configured under `review.*` in `.entire/settings.json`, only the **alphabetically first** agent actually launches. A user who has configured both `claude-code` and `codex` sees only `claude-code` spawn, with no interactive way to pick a different one unless they remember to pass `--agent NAME`.\n\nThe Phase 1 picker work (this PR's first 5 commits) is already multi-agent-aware at *configure* time — `runReviewConfigPicker` iterates every installed + eligible agent and saves a per-agent `ReviewConfig`. The gap is at *spawn* time: `selectReviewAgent(review, override)` silently defaults to the alphabetical-first key when `override == \"\"`.\n\nUsers discover this by running `entire review`, seeing Claude spawn every time, and only realizing Codex was available if they re-read the help text for `--agent`.\n\n## Goals\n\n1. When a user has more than one agent configured (and both have hooks installed), `entire review` prompts interactively: *\"Which agent should run this review?\"*\n2. The `--agent NAME` flag still works and skips the prompt (scripting path).\n3. Single-agent configs still fast-path — no picker in the one-choice case.\n4. Consistent with Phase 1's design principle: the silent-failure shape should be impossible, without adding friction to the common path.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Multi-select (running >1 agent) — that's Sub-phase 2.2.\n- Parallel spawn, live panes, Ctrl+O inspection, aggregated summary, AI synthesis — Sub-phases 2.2 through 2.4.\n- Remembering the last-picked agent across runs — keep it stateless; `--agent` is the scripting escape hatch.\n- Reordering the re-run guard vs. agent picker. The guard is agent-agnostic and fires after selection; that order is unchanged.\n\n## Architecture overview\n\nTwo small changes in `cmd/entire/cli/review.go`:\n\n1. A new `promptForAgent(ctx, eligible []string, out io.Writer) (string, error)` helper that runs a `huh.Select` over the eligible agent names and returns the pick.\n2. Insert a pre-check in `runReview` *before* `selectReviewAgent`:\n\n```\neligibleConfigured := configured ∩ hooks-installed\n\nif agentOverride == \"\" && len(eligibleConfigured) > 1:\n    agentOverride = promptForAgent(ctx, eligibleConfigured, out)\n\n// Fall through to existing selectReviewAgent(review, agentOverride)\n```\n\nThe rest of `runReview` is untouched. `selectReviewAgent` is unchanged — when `agentOverride` is non-empty (either from the flag or from the new picker), it resolves the config for that agent and returns; otherwise it falls back to alphabetical-first, which now only happens when `len(eligibleConfigured) == 1`.\n\n## Eligibility filter\n\n`eligibleConfigured` is computed once in `runReview`, before the picker:\n\n```go\nhookInstalled := GetAgentsWithHooksInstalled(ctx) // []types.AgentName, existing\nvar eligible []string\nfor name := range s.Review {\n    if cfg := s.Review[name]; cfg.IsZero() { continue }\n    if !slices.Contains(hookInstalled, types.AgentName(name)) { continue }\n    eligible = append(eligible, name)\n}\nsort.Strings(eligible)\n```\n\nThis replaces the post-selection hooks-installed gate *as a filter input*, but the gate itself stays as the runtime backstop for the `--agent NAME` path (`NAME` could point at a configured-but-hookless agent).\n\n## Picker UX\n\nLabel per option derived from the agent's `ReviewConfig`:\n\n- `len(cfg.Skills) > 0` → `\"<agent-name>   (N skills configured)\"` where N = `len(cfg.Skills)`\n- `len(cfg.Skills) == 0 && cfg.Prompt != \"\"` → `\"<agent-name>   (prompt-only)\"`\n- (Other combinations shouldn't occur — `selectReviewAgent` already rejects `cfg.IsZero()`.)\n\nDefault selection: alphabetically-first. This preserves the pre-2.1 silent-default behavior for Enter-through users — anyone used to pressing Enter and getting Claude still gets Claude.\n\nCtrl+C: clean exit, silent error, no pending marker written. Same pattern as the existing `confirmFirstRunSetup` cancel path.\n\nTitle: `\"Which agent should run this review?\"`. No subtitle; the option labels are self-explanatory.\n\n## Error cases\n\n| Condition | Behavior |\n|---|---|\n| 0 eligible (none configured OR none with hooks) | Existing error path: \"Hooks are not installed for … Run `entire configure --agent X` first, or remove X from review settings.\" No change. |\n| 1 eligible | Skip picker. Spawn that agent. No interactive prompt. |\n| 2+ eligible, `--agent NAME` passed | Skip picker. Validate NAME is in eligibleConfigured; error listing alternatives if not (existing behavior in `selectReviewAgent`). |\n| 2+ eligible, no flag | Picker fires. |\n| User cancels picker (Ctrl+C / Esc) | Return silent error, exit non-zero, no marker. |\n\n## Testing strategy\n\nFour unit tests in `cmd/entire/cli/review_test.go`:\n\n- `TestPromptForAgent_SingleEligibleSkipsPicker` — drive `runReview` with 1 configured + hooks; assert `promptForAgent` is not called (via a test-local hook or by asserting marker is written without interactive input).\n- `TestPromptForAgent_FlagOverrideSkipsPicker` — 2 configured, `--agent codex` flag → marker `AgentName == codex`, no interactive prompt needed.\n- `TestPromptForAgent_FlagOverrideMustBeEligibleAgent` — 2 configured, `--agent bogus` → error listing eligible names.\n- `TestRunReview_MultiAgentNoFlagTriggersPicker` — harder to unit-test because of the TUI, but we can assert the *pre-picker state*: if `promptForAgent` were called, it'd see the right eligible slice. Factor it into a function that takes a `selectFn` parameter we can stub.\n\nThe interactive TUI path itself isn't unit-testable headlessly (same as Phase 1's config picker). Manual smoke during `entire review` with 2+ configured agents is how the real UX gets exercised.\n\nNo integration tests needed — `entire review` integration scaffolding in `cmd/entire/cli/integration_test/review_test.go` already covers the marker→adoption→condensation loop. This sub-phase changes only which `agentName` gets into the marker, which is observable via existing assertions.\n\n## Files touched\n\n- Modify: `cmd/entire/cli/review.go` — add `promptForAgent`, compute `eligibleConfigured`, wire the pre-check into `runReview`.\n- Modify: `cmd/entire/cli/review_test.go` — four new tests.\n\nEstimated diff: ~120 lines across 2 files. One commit.\n\n## Follow-ups (sub-phases 2.2, 2.3, 2.4)\n\nAll deferred to their own specs once 2.1 ships:\n\n- **2.2** Multi-select + parallel headless spawn + status-table TUI (`claude --print` / `codex exec` / etc., bubbletea Model per agent row).\n- **2.3** Ctrl+O drill-in + aggregated summary at end.\n- **2.4** AI-synthesized cross-agent verdict as optional final synthesizer.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3074,"cache_read_input_tokens":440516,"output_tokens":3343,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3074,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":3343,"cache_read_input_tokens":440516,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3074