{"parentUuid":"4e7de95b-137d-48f0-bdf7-803515dd0644","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01EwpH9hnEZEGQFAvMiZgsCA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Server at **http://localhost:55470**. First question is conceptual, so terminal:\n\n**Question 1 — scope / the \"parallel\" part.**\n\nYour Phase 2 enumeration says \"parallel headless spawn + status table\" — \"parallel\" is front-and-center. But there's a real engineering cliff between the three interpretations:\n\n- **A. \"Parallel under the hood, sequentially rendered\" — easiest.** Fire all agents as goroutines. Wait for each in order. Status row updates as each one actually flips state. User sees A=running, then A=done B=running, then A=done B=done etc. No bubbletea compositor: just a progressive `fmt.Println` log. Ships fast. **But the user experience is barely different from sequential**, since events still serialize in display.\n\n- **B. \"Parallel execution + live static table\" — standard.** Goroutines spawn all N agents simultaneously. A bubbletea `tea.Model` renders a status table (one row per agent) and updates on every state change. No streaming stdout — just row state (queued / running / done / failed), duration counter, token counters if captured. Ctrl+C kills all subprocesses. **This is the typical \"parallel agents dashboard\" UX** and what most people mean by 2.2.\n\n- **C. \"Parallel + live table + streaming output preview\" — biggest.** Each row shows the most recent line of that agent's stdout in a truncated form, updating live. Still no Ctrl+O drill-in (that's 2.3). But each row has a 60-char preview that tails the agent's output. Adds significant complexity (per-agent stdout tailing goroutine, line-buffer per row, terminal width handling).\n\nMy strong lean: **B**. It's the canonical \"pr-review-toolkit subagent display\" shape you referenced — status rows with spinners + state, no scrollback. C is tempting but blurs the 2.2/2.3 