{"parentUuid":"ca760a99-bfcf-43bf-9156-4c8cfb1a439b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01XSTptpHRKRVxU9jCTwJLdk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C it is — table + streaming preview. Biggest engineering lift but highest visual payoff. Let me tell you what the hardest unknowns still are.\n\n**Question 2 — checkpoint / marker model for multi-agent runs.**\n\nThis blocks most of the implementation work. Today's Phase 1 + 2.1 code assumes *one agent per run*: one pending-review marker under `.git/entire-sessions/review-pending.json`, one session gets tagged via `adoptPendingReviewMarkerInto`, one checkpoint on `entire/checkpoints/v1`. Multi-agent breaks this three ways: marker contention (one file, N readers), session-tagging races, and \"how does the checkpoint look in `git log` when 3 agents reviewed the same commit?\"\n\nThree models:\n\n- **A. One marker per agent, one checkpoint per agent.** Write `review-pending-<agent>.json` for each selected agent. Each agent's hook adopts its own scoped marker (scoping: worktree + agent, same as today). Each agent's session produces its own `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer in the final commit. Result: the user commit ends up with **N `Entire-Checkpoint` trailers**, one per agent, each pointing at its own condensed metadata on `entire/checkpoints/v1`. Simplest mental model, no shared state, but creates new \"multiple trailers on one commit\" surface that tooling downstream (the `HasReview` check, `entire status`, `entire explain`) needs to understand.\n\n- **B. One shared marker, one multi-session checkpoint.** Extend the marker to carry `AgentNames []string` instead of a single name. Each agent's hook adopts the marker but *doesn't clear it* until the last agent adopts (refcount or \"all adopted?\" check). Condensation writes one checkpoint with N sessions nested under it (PR #993 / #1009 already has the shape for multi-session checkpoints via `CommittedMetadata`'s numbered-subfolder layout). Single `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer on the user commit. More integration work on the marker/hook path; no new tooling surface downstream.\n\n- **C. Hybrid: per-agent markers during run, merged checkpoint on condense.** N markers live during the run (so hook adoption is unambiguous). On the next `git commit`, the condense step detects N markers adopted against the same HEAD and merges them into one shared checkpoint with N sessions. User commit gets one trailer. Complexity sits in the condense path.\n\n**My lean: B**, despite the marker-refcount complexity. Reasons:\n1. PR #993's checkpoint model already supports multi-session checkpoints natively (`session_ids []`, `session_count`, numbered subfolders). We'd be *using* existing infra rather than inventing a new \"N trailers\" concept.\n2. `entire status` and `entire explain` already handle `HasReview` as a single bool on the summary — A would need them to aggregate across N trailers.\n3. `ReviewSkills`/`ReviewPrompt` on `CommittedMetadata` would become per-session fields under the shared checkpoint, which is exactly what \"3 agents ran 3 different skill sets against the same commit\" wants.\n\nA is easier to build but creates technical debt for every downstream consumer. C is a reasonable middle ground but the \"merge on condense\" logic is subtle.\n\nWhich? Or is there a fourth framing I'm missing (e.g., don't checkpoint multi-agent runs at all and treat them as ephemeral)?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":267,"cache_read_input_tokens":514998,"output_tokens":1753,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":267},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":1753,"cache_read_input_tokens":514998,"cache_creation_input_tokens":267,"cache_creation":{"ephemer