{"parentUuid":"61dd1cf2-a762-4da2-a9a6-cee21487c09d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01UftQcMjz4Dmifw32nZ1ESV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Locked. Next clarifying round is smaller — two related UX decisions about failure + cancel.\n\n**Question 4 — failure + cancel semantics.**\n\nMy proposed defaults (common-sense for a parallel dashboard; tell me if anything feels wrong):\n\n**Failure handling — per-agent independent.** One agent exits non-zero, others keep going. Its row shows `✗ failed (exit 1)` + duration; the rest continue. Final TUI state makes clear which succeeded vs failed. No prompt, no fail-fast. Rationale: in a parallel review, a codex failure shouldn't cost you the claude and gemini results.\n\n**Cancel — graceful then forceful on second Ctrl+C.**\n- First Ctrl+C → send SIGTERM to all running subprocesses, show `⊘ cancelling…` on running rows, wait up to ~5s.\n- Second Ctrl+C within that window → SIGKILL.\n- On exit, clean up the shared pending-review marker so a subsequent run starts fresh (no ghost marker waiting for hooks that'll never fire).\n\n**Partial completion note.** If some agents finished and some were cancelled, the finished ones DID fire their UserPromptSubmit hooks and their sessions got tagged. That's fine — when the user later commits, those agents' sessions condense normally into a checkpoint that just happens to have fewer nested sessions than originally launched. The cancelled ones leave no session state (they exited before UserPromptSubmit fired). No manual cleanup needed.\n\nAny objection to those defaults? If you're OK with all three, one more question left (what happens after all agents finish — exit immediately vs pause on completion screen), then I present the full design for your approval."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2544,"cache_read_input_tokens":530100,