led\" — but for `AgentRunCancelled`, the error will typically be `context.Canceled`/SIGTERM-exit. Spec doesn't say whether the completion dump's `if result.Err != nil` branch (line 337) should print cancellation errors. The Cancelled output path at line 338-339 already says \"(cancelled)\" — the `Err != nil` branch applies only to Failed. Tighten: \"Err populated for Failed only; Cancelled uses Status alone.\" Minor, but avoids double-printing on cancel.\n\n2. **[Runtime orchestration, line 266-270] Goroutine-safety gap in `teeWriter`.** Spec says `cmd.Stdout = teeWriter(&buf, previewChan)` — the same `buf` is later read at line 270 (`FinalOutput: buf`) after `cmd.Run()` returns on the same goroutine, so that's fine. But the orchestrator later reads `run.FinalOutput` on the main goroutine (line 340). Spec should state that the completion-dump loop runs **after** `program.Run()` returns and **after** all task goroutines have sent their terminal `agentStateMsg` — i.e., the `FinalOutput` happens-before is established via the terminal-msg channel. Implicit in the lifecycle but worth making explicit to avoid a future race.\n\n3. **[Picker dispatch, line 371-388] When `runnable < 2 && eligible >= 2`, the fallback passes full `eligible` to `promptForAgentFn`.** Correct choice, but the user now sees TTY-only agents in the single-select list with no indication that multi-agent wasn't offered. Consider noting in the picker that when this fallback triggers, a one-line hint (\"multi-agent unavailable: only N agents support headless mode\") would aid UX. Advisory.\n\n4. **[Cancellation cascade, line 284-290] Second-Ctrl+C SIGKILL bypass is under-specified.** Spec says \"second Ctrl+C within the window → immediate SIGKILL bypass.\" But Go's default signal handler cancels ctx on first Ctrl+C and **exits the process** on the second by default — orchestrator must explicitly `signal.Notify` on SIGINT to intercept both presses. Not called out. Also, \"within the window\" is ambiguous: within the 5s watchdog window, or any time after first press? Minor; implementation will clarify, but worth one sentence.\n\n5. **[Hook adoption pseudocode, line 186] Worktree-scoping not shown.** Phase 1/2.1's adoption gates on `WorktreePath` match (per CLAUDE.md \"marker is only adopted by a session whose worktree matches\"). The multi-agent branch pseudocode omits this check — should explicitly say the worktree check still applies before the `AgentNames` membership test. Without it, a marker from worktree A could be adopted by sessions in worktree B.\n\n6. **[Chunking, line 442-446] Chunk 2 dispatch needs Chunk 1 to compile.** Correct per the question — and the table implicitly orders Chunk 1 before Chunk 2. One sentence explicitly stating \"Chunk 1 must land before Chunk 2 (dispatch type-checks `HeadlessLauncher`)\" would prevent reordering in review.\n\n**Scope / YAGNI:** No 2.3/2.4 bleed detected. Live token counter, Ctrl+O, aggregated summary, AI synthesis all correctly in Non-goals (lines 28-35) and Follow-ups (lines 459-463). Per-agent retry correctly deferred.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\n- Add one sentence in §Hook adoption confirming worktree-path guard still applies in the multi-agent branch.\n- Add one sentence in §Cancellation stating `signal.Notify(SIGINT)` is required to intercept both presses.\n- Clarify `Err` is Failed-only; `Cancelled` uses `Status` alone for dump branching.\n- State ordering constraint \"Chunk 1 before Chunk 2\" in the chunking section.\n\nArchitecture (6 units), testability, coverage, and completion-dump behavior all look sound. Spec is ready for planning once the five issues above are clarified — none are blockers."}],"totalDurationMs":36980,"totalTokens":42348,"totalToolUseCount":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15738,"cache_read_input_tokens":25087,"output_tokens":1522,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tok