{"parentUuid":"6b4c6b6b-e809-45ed-81e3-b99e5dba5d82","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"7e04f256-eb7d-47e9-8611-45eaf4a91a82","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/peytonmontei/.claude/plugins/cache/superpowers-marketplace/superpowers/5.0.2/skills/writing-plans\n\n# Writing Plans\n\n## Overview\n\nWrite comprehensive implementation plans assuming the engineer has zero context for our codebase and questionable taste. Document everything they need to know: which files to touch for each task, code, testing, docs they might need to check, how to test it. Give them the whole plan as bite-sized tasks. DRY. YAGNI. TDD. Frequent commits.\n\nAssume they are a skilled developer, but know almost nothing about our toolset or problem domain. Assume they don't know good test design very well.\n\n**Announce at start:** \"I'm using the writing-plans skill to create the implementation plan.\"\n\n**Context:** This should be run in a dedicated worktree (created by brainstorming skill).\n\n**Save plans to:** `docs/superpowers/plans/YYYY-MM-DD-<feature-name>.md`\n- (User preferences for plan location override this default)\n\n## Scope Check\n\nIf the spec covers multiple independent subsystems, it should have been broken into sub-project specs during brainstorming. If it wasn't, suggest breaking this into separate plans — one per subsystem. Each plan should produce working, testable software on its own.\n\n## File Structure\n\nBefore defining tasks, map out which files will be created or modified and what each one is responsible for. This is where decomposition decisions get locked in.\n\n- Design units with clear boundaries and well-defined interfaces. Each file should have one clear responsibility.\n- You reason best about code you can hold in context at once, and your edits are more reliable when files are focused. Prefer smaller, focused files over large ones that do too much.\n- Files that change together should live together. Split by responsibility, not by technical layer.\n- In existing codebases, follow established patterns. If the codebase uses large files, don't unilaterally restructure - but if a file you're modifying has grown unwieldy, including a split in the plan is reasonable.\n\nThis structure informs the task decomposition. Each task should produce self-contained changes that make sense independently.\n\n## Bite-Sized Task Granularity\n\n**Each step is one action (2-5 minutes):**\n- \"Write the failing test\" - step\n- \"Run it to make sure it fails\" - step\n- \"Implement the minimal code to make the test pass\" - step\n- \"Run the tests and make sure they pass\" - step\n- \"Commit\" - step\n\n## Plan Document Header\n\n**Every plan MUST start with this header:**\n\n```markdown\n# [Feature Name] Implementation Plan\n\n> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (if subagents available) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.\n\n**Goal:** [One sentence describing what this builds]\n\n**Architecture:** [2-3 sentences about approach]\n\n**Tech Stack:** [Key technologies/libraries]\n\n---\n```\n\n## Task Structure\n\n````markdown\n### Task N: [Component Name]\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `exact/path/to/file.py`\n- Modify: `exact/path/to/existing.py:123-145`\n- Test: `tests/exact/path/to/test.py`\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the failing test**\n\n```python\ndef test_specific_behavior():\n    result = function(input)\n    assert result == expected\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Run test to verify it fails**\n\nRun: `pytest tests/path/test.py::test_name -v`\nExpected: FAIL with \"function not defined\"\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Write minimal implementation**\n\n```python\ndef function(input):\n    return expected\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Run test to verify it passes**\n\nRun: `pytest tests/path/test.py::test_name -v`\nExpected: PASS\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Commit**\n\n```bash\ngit add tests/path/test.py src/path/file.py\ngit commit -m \"feat: add specific feature\"\n```\n````\n\n## Remember\n- Exact file paths always\n- Complete code in plan (not \"add validation\")\n- Exact commands with expected output\n- Reference relevant skills with @ syntax\n- DRY, YAGNI, TDD, frequent commits\n\n## Plan Review Loop\n\nAfter completing each chunk of the plan:\n\n1. Dispatch plan-document-reviewer subagent (see plan-document-reviewer-prompt.md) with precisely crafted review context — never your session history. This keeps the reviewer focused on the plan, not your thought process.\n   - Provide: chunk content, path to spec document\n2. If ❌ Issues Found:\n   - Fix the issues in the chunk\n   - Re-dispatch reviewer for that chunk\n   - Repeat until ✅ Approved\n3. If ✅ Approved: proceed to next chunk (or execution handoff if last chunk)\n\n**Chunk boundaries:** Use `## Chunk N: <name>` headings to delimit chunks. Each chunk should be ≤1000 lines and logically self-contained.\n\n**Review loop guidance:**\n- Same agent that wrote the plan fixes it (preserves context)\n- If loop exceeds 5 iterations, surface to human for guidance\n- Reviewers are advisory - explain disagreements if you believe feedback is incorrect\n\n## Execution Handoff\n\nAfter saving the plan:\n\n**\"Plan complete and saved to `docs/superpowers/plans/<filename>.md`. Ready to execute?\"**\n\n**Execution path depends on harness capabilities:**\n\n**If harness has subagents (Claude Code, etc.):**\n- **REQUIRED:** Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development\n- Do NOT offer a choice - subagent-driven is the standard approach\n- Fresh subagent per task + two-stage review\n\n**If harness does NOT have subagents:**\n- Execute plan in current session using superpowers:executing-plans\n- Batch execution with checkpoints for review\n\n\nARGUMENTS: Produce the implementation plan for `entire review` Sub-phase 2.2 (multi-agent parallel spawn + status TUI).\n\n**Spec:** `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-22-entire-review-multi-agent-parallel-design.md` (uncommitted, under gitignored `docs/superpowers/`).\n\n**Branch strategy:** Working branch `feat/entire-review-multi-agent` already checked out, tracking `origin/feat/entire-review` at tip `874f9a4ba`. Chunks commit directly to this branch (no new branch per chunk). The plan should assume this branch state — all of Phase 1 + 2.1 picker work is present (including Soph's recent picker fixes from `fe41ea5fe`, `38c7d5c38`, `40681ab7b`, `874f9a4ba`), and the `skilldiscovery` package is still in place (simplification is parked on a separate local branch and doesn't conflict).\n\n**Plan shape: 4 chunks, one commit each, in strict order** (type-check dependencies):\n\n1. **Chunk 1** — `HeadlessLauncher` capability interface + 3-5 per-agent implementations (start with claudecode, codex, geminicli; cursor + copilot-cli land in follow-up commits). Each adapter reuses existing headless invocation patterns from the repo's `GenerateText` flow. Tests.\n2. **Chunk 2** — Multi-select picker (`promptForAgents`) + `runReviewDeps` extensions (`promptForAgentsFn`, `runMultiAgentFn` injection hooks) + dispatch logic in `runReview` that routes to multi-agent when len(selected) ≥ 2 and falls back to 2.1 single-agent otherwise. Tests stub `runMultiAgentFn` to verify dispatch without actual subprocesses or bubbletea.\n3. **Chunk 3** — `multiReviewOrchestrator` in new file `review_multi.go` — goroutine-per-agent spawn using `HeadlessLauncher`, shared pending-review marker with `AgentNames []string`, hook adoption multi-agent branch with existing worktree + SHA guards, `signal.Notify(sigCh, syscall.SIGINT)` cancellation cascade (SIGTERM → 5s watchdog → SIGKILL or second-Ctrl+C bypass), `MultiRunResult` post-exit transcript-path + `CalculateTokenUsage` resolution. Tests via fake `HeadlessLauncher` that returns a controllable `*exec.Cmd`.\n4. **Chunk 4** — `reviewTUIModel` in new file `review_tui.go` — bubbletea `tea.Model` with per-agent rows (status, duration, tokens, preview), message-driven Update (agentStateMsg, agentPreviewMsg, cancelMsg, tickMsg), preview plumbing with ANSI-strip + width-truncate + rate-limit, final completion dump of per-agent FinalOutput + summary line + transcript paths. Tests via `github.com/charmbracelet/x/exp/teatest` for headless TUI model testing.\n\n**Key constraints the plan must honor:**\n- Do NOT modify `selectReviewAgent` or 2.1's `promptForAgent` — they stay exactly as they are for the single-agent path.\n- Do NOT touch condensation / checkpoint-writing code — Phase 1's multi-session infrastructure (`session_count`, numbered subfolders) already supports what 2.2 produces when the user runs `git commit` later.\n- Hook adoption for the new multi-agent marker MUST keep Phase 1/2.1 worktree-path and SHA-drift guards in place before the `AgentNames` membership check.\n- Orchestrator owns marker lifecycle — `defer ClearPendingReviewMarker(ctx)`. Hooks do NOT clear.\n- First Ctrl+C → SIGTERM on Unix; Windows gets SIGKILL immediately (documented as non-goal).\n- All tests use `t.Parallel()` unless they call `t.Setenv` / `t.Chdir`.\n- `NewAccessibleForm` for every new huh form.\n- Lint conventions: err113, perfsprint, gosec G304, unparam, inamedparam (carry forward from Phase 1 / 2.1 learnings).\n\n**Testing approach the plan must specify:**\n- Unit tests in `cli/review_test.go` for picker dispatch + injection hook behavior.\n- Unit tests in new `cli/review_multi_test.go` for orchestrator with fake `HeadlessLauncher`.\n- Unit tests in new `cli/review_tui_test.go` via `teatest` for Model behavior.\n- Unit tests in `cli/lifecycle_test.go` for hook-adoption multi-agent branch.\n- NO new E2E tests — existing Vogon canary continues to cover spawn→adopt→commit.\n\n**Commit messages (verbatim, one per chunk):**\n1. `Add HeadlessLauncher capability interface and per-agent implementations`\n2. `Add multi-select picker and dispatch to multi-agent path`\n3. `Add multiReviewOrchestrator with shared marker and cancellation cascade`\n4. `Add reviewTUIModel with live status table and completion dump`\n\n**CI gate for each chunk:** `mise run fmt && mise run lint && mise run check && mise run dup` all green before commit.\n\n**Do NOT start execution.** The plan is for a separate execution session. After writing the plan and running the plan-document-reviewer loop, surface the plan to the user for their review before invoking subagent-driven-development."}]},"isMeta":true,"uuid":"8bf4d3ac-572e-4bc2-8afe-6a00486a1669","timestamp":"2026-04-23T17:54:04.928Z","sourceToolUseID":"toolu_012xdYUBQzLukEWLkptfEoR5","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/entire-review","sessionId":"32ff705a-db92-4346-a9c8-70b631ba581d","version":"2.1.117","gitBranch":"feat/entire-review-multi-agent"}
